If you haven't yet touched (or attached) your ribber ... <b>hold your horses! </b> <i class="fa fa-smile-o aria-hidden="true"></i><br>If you've been able to follow the instructions, cast on and confirm that the ribber is set up correctly and working, you should be good.  We aren't going to create a wardrobe in ribbing (yet <i class="fa fa-smile-o aria-hidden="true"></i>
) but <b>the goal is to just take some concentrated time to play and gain confidence.</b><br>With that being said, I normally tell newer knitters to master the single bed first.  Learn a couple of cast on methods, be comfortable with stitch patterning ... learn shaping techniques ... learn about what yarns you  like and work best with your machine ... make a few things before attempting the ribber.  With the ribber there are so many more things going on than with just the single bed. <br>It's like learning to play the piano.  Start with your dominant hand and pick out a few notes.  Learn the layout of the keys.  Learn to read the music. Practice reading the music and playing without looking .... then add the other hand ....   at some point your feet get in the act. (OK - you can tell that I don't teach piano, but you get the idea) <br><b>With a double bed setup, you need to keep track of 2 machines and UNDERSTAND what's going on with BOTH of them (at the same time).</b><br>If you feel you aren't for the Knit-along right now, you might want to take time to watch the videos and start getting a picture of what the ribber is all about. <b> It's not hard!</b>  But we don't want to cause any tears because you aren't ready.<br>Although participating "live" gives you access to the forum and hopefully encourages you to MAKE TIME to knit, <b>the content will be available in the Learning Library for you to follow later (when you are ready)</b><br><b>PS</b><br>
<em>If this initial Knit-along seems to basic for you ... we have more scheduled.  The whole topic of using your ribber is HUGE! (and we couldn't start with double bed jacquard in good conscience <i class="fa fa-smile-o aria-hidden="true"></i>)</em> Stay tuned!
